  "query_preview": "../Wednesday through Saturday/... \nThe front that brought heavy rain and strong winds on Tuesday will\nhave all but cleared the southern panhandle by daybreak\nWednesday. In its wake, persistent onshore flow will continue\nthrough Wednesday and into Thursday as the low lingers and slowly\nweakens in the northern Gulf of Alaska. Subtle shortwaves and \nupper troughs swinging around this low will keep higher \nprecipitation chances with moderate to heavy showers and gusty \nwinds across SEAK through Thursday. Behind the front, snow levels\nfall to 1,000\u20132,000 ft on Wednesday over the northern panhandle. \nSnow showers will be possible along the Klondike Highway and upper\nreaches of the Haines Highway through Thursday, though any \naccumulation will remain light. As the low weakens and gradient \nrelaxes behind the frontal passage, gusty southerly winds will \nslowly diminish through Thursday. The northern inner channels and\ninto Haines and Skagway could still be quite gusty on Wednesday \nas the north to south gradient remains a bit tighter, and the High\nWind Watch remains in effect of Skagway. As the Gulf low weakens \nfurther and shifts southeast toward the end of the week, showers \nwill lighten and end from north to south Thursday night into \nFriday across SEAK. Upper-level ridging makes builds east across \nMainland Alaska by late week, with SEAK on the eastern edge of the\nridge. Ensemble and deterministic guidance generally showing high\npressure building in the wake of the departing low, with clearing\nskies and drier offshore flow developing Friday and continuing \ninto the early weekend. Daytime highs will remain in the 40s \nthroughout the period, with overnight lows in the mid 30s to low \n40s.",
